            <ArticleTitle>Coronary artery-left ventricle fistula after PTCA: diagnosed by contrast echocardiography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>729-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A coronary artery fistula is a rare complication following percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA). It may result in communication with either the left or right ventricle. Previous reported cases were diagnosed by coronary angiography using contrast medium. We present a case with a septal coronary artery fistula to the left ventricle following PTCA that was diagnosed by contrast echocardiography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
